(D6 — Discrepancy 6 repro, HISTORICAL, 0.4.14 and earlier. The cyclic-list
 example at LANGUAGE.md:2431-2434 (0.4.14 numbering) records its output as
 "(prints: [[...]] then cyclic)". The compiler printed 64 opening brackets,
 `...`, 64 closing brackets, then `cyclic`. The surrounding PROSE
 (2293-2297) was exact and correct; only the example's inline comment was
 abbreviated. Recorded because a leaf that asserted the example's literal
 text would manufacture a false finding on every run.

 MOOT as of 0.4.15 (#111): `append x to x.` no longer builds a
 self-referential list — nesting always copies now — so this exact program
 prints `[[]]` and never reaches the depth cap or the error handler. The
 discrepancy is resolved (see collections-a.md, Discrepancy 6): there is no
 longer a "(prints: …)" comment on this example to be abbreviated or
 verbatim, because the example's own documented output changed. Retained
 as the historical record of the pre-#111 behavior, not as a
 currently-passing claim. See LST-32.vox and LST-33 (withdrawn).
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D6.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   [[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[...]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]
   cyclic
)
a list called x is [].
append x to x.
print x.
